requirements.requirement:
Every business word gets one written meaning: what counts, which dates, which currency, and what to do when a value is missing. Today we name the first set of words and the order in which the rest follow, so the day stays honest about its size.

Source locator: DAYS[1].blocks[0]
{
  "what": "Every business word gets one written meaning: what counts, which dates, which currency, and what to do when a value is missing. Today we name the first set of words and the order in which the rest follow, so the day stays honest about its size.",
  "piece": "meaning",
  "architectureNodes": "word=A business word: sale, customer, paid invoice;def=One written meaning: what counts, which dates, which currency|miss=What to do when a value is missing|gov=Invoice fields follow the government e-invoice layout;dict=The dictionary, first set: business, customer, supplier, item, invoice, payment, account;later=Later days add: corrections, permissions, actions",
  "architectureEdges": "word>def;def>dict;miss>dict;gov>dict;dict>~later:in written order",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "Every business uses the same words in its own way. So each word the companion uses gets one written meaning in the dictionary. A meaning says what counts, which dates, which currency, and what to do when a value is missing. We do not try to define the whole world today. The first set is eight things: the business, customer, supplier, item, invoice, invoice line, payment and account. Corrections, permissions and actions join on later days, and that order is written down."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Build room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Core, start day 2 block 1 with Munim: the dictionary format for the local profile, as data. Name the first normative subset in docs/dictionary.md and build only that today. Object types: Business, Customer, Supplier, Item, Invoice, InvoiceLine, Payment, Account. Constructs: typed properties, relationships, measures, periods, provenance. Every money value has exact decimals and a currency, every quantity has a unit, every field states one of the five missing-value states (absent, null, unknown, withheld, not applicable). Anchor the Invoice and InvoiceLine fields on the GST e-invoice schema (Form GST INV-01): map field by field and list every field where we need more or less. Add an exchange-rate object and one rule: an amount in another currency is converted at the rate of its document date from a rate table, the rate used is on the receipt, and an amount with no rate is reported as not counted, never guessed. In the same document, write the order in which the rest follows: events and corrections on day 3, source scope on day 4, measures for analysis on day 5, policies and markings on day 6, actions on day 7. Post the subset list and the INV-01 difference list. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "fleet",
      "do": "Core and Munim reply with the list. While they work, open the diagram and walk it: a word, its written meaning, what to do when a value is missing, the first set. Read two meanings aloud in plain words, for example paid invoice and customer."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "When Core reports, paste:",
      "prompt": "Core, show on screen the meaning of 'paid invoice' as the owner will read it, in plain words, and the same meaning as data. Then show one dollar invoice from the demo shop: the rate used, the date of the rate, and what the receipt says. Then show the dollar invoice whose date has no rate in the table, and what the answer says about it.",
      "say": "A meaning you cannot read is a meaning you cannot correct. And when a rate is missing, it says so. It does not make one up."
    }
  ],
  "gapIds": [
    "ONT-27",
    "ONT-31",
    "INT-61"
  ]
}

Two lakh fifty thousand, Dr and Cr after a number, the first of April written day first, a year that runs April to March, and words like udhaar and khata. One named set of reading rules and one word list in Hindi and English, used by every recipe and every question.

Source locator: DAYS[1].blocks[1]
{
  "what": "Two lakh fifty thousand, Dr and Cr after a number, the first of April written day first, a year that runs April to March, and words like udhaar and khata. One named set of reading rules and one word list in Hindi and English, used by every recipe and every question.",
  "piece": "mapping",
  "architectureNodes": "in=What people write and say: 2,50,000 or dhai lakh or 01-04-2026 or udhaar;rules=India reading rules: lakh and crore, Dr and Cr, day first, April to March|alias=Word lists in Hindi and English;text=Shown back as text, for your yes|unk=Unknown word: logged, never guessed",
  "architectureEdges": "in>rules;in>alias;rules>text;alias>text;alias>~unk:no match",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "One minute on this. Indian books do not look like American books. We write two lakh fifty thousand with the commas in different places. We write Dr and Cr after a number. The first of April is written day first, and the year runs April to March. People say udhaar, khata and party. So there is one set of India reading rules and one word list, and every recipe uses them. If you say dhai lakh, it shows two point five lakh rupees as text and waits for your yes."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Build room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Mapper-2, start day 2 block 2 with Munim, Brain and Voice. (1) A named lexical profile, in.default: Indian digit grouping, lakh and crore, Dr and Cr suffixes, negative amounts in brackets, day-first dates, the April to March financial year, common encodings. Every India recipe refers to it by name. (2) Alias packs in Hindi and English shipped with each core pack: udhaar, khata, party, debtor, creditor, bikri, vasooli and the like, each bound to a definition or an operation. An unknown business word is logged as an event in the learning log and is never guessed. (3) An India locale test set of at least 60 cases in tests/conformance/india-locale, including spoken forms such as dhai lakh, sava lakh and paune do crore. Voice: every spoken amount is shown as text and confirmed before anything runs. (4) One table in docs/languages.md that states the language per layer: screen text, voice, definition names and aliases, reports, and the data itself, which is kept as written in any script. Most of this finishes overnight: say what is done today. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "Today's proof is one line in the test bench score in Prove the day: how many of the India cases pass. The full set is checked tomorrow morning. If a case fails, read it aloud.",
      "say": "One wrong reading of a spoken amount and the promise breaks at the first step. So it always shows the amount as text first."
    }
  ],
  "gapIds": [
    "ONT-20",
    "ONT-32",
    "IND-11",
    "IND-08"
  ]
}

Chausar's own way of writing a recipe: this column in that system means this word in your dictionary. A recipe says which company it is for, which blanks are filled in for each business, which checks must pass, and when to stop and ask. It is designed today, because day 1 already mapped a sheet and day 4 needs it for fifty systems.

Source locator: DAYS[1].blocks[2]
{
  "what": "Chausar's own way of writing a recipe: this column in that system means this word in your dictionary. A recipe says which company it is for, which blanks are filled in for each business, which checks must pass, and when to stop and ask. It is designed today, because day 1 already mapped a sheet and day 4 needs it for fifty systems.",
  "piece": "mapping",
  "architectureNodes": "sys=A sheet or system with its own column names|who=Which company and which GST number|blank=Blanks filled in per business, each with a type|read=India reading rules;map=The recipe: this column means this word, with unit, currency and period;chk=Checks against the source's own totals|stop=Not sure: stop and give the reason|extra=Columns nobody mapped go on a list;facts=Facts in your dictionary's words",
  "architectureEdges": "sys>map;who>map;blank>map;read>map;map>chk;map>~stop:if unsure;map>extra;chk>facts:pass",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "Yesterday the meaning of each column in the sales sheet was written by hand. That does not stretch to Tally, Zoho and a bank. So today we design the mapping language. A recipe written in it says six things. Which company and which GST number it is for. Which blanks must be filled in for each business, and what type each blank is. How each column becomes a word in the dictionary, with its unit, currency and period. Which checks must pass against the source's own totals. When to stop and give a reason instead of guessing. And what happens to columns nobody mapped: they go on a list, they are not thrown away. We own this frame. For the small formulas inside a recipe we borrow a ready-made formula language, and we test two today."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Build room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Mapper-1, start day 2 block 3 with Core, Pipeline and Atlas: the mapping language, version 0.1, as a specification plus a compiler front end with tests. The frame is ours. Constructs, all required: identity header (template id, template version, language version, target definition revisions); source block (system, route, artifact, grain); scope (company, branch, tax registration, fiscal calendar, base currency, time zone), one recipe instance per scope; typed bindings, each with kind (scalar, enum map, account classification, lookup table, calendar, custom field), discovery method (probe, model proposal, user question), default, reviewer, provenance and recheck rule; lexical profile by name; map (identity keys, field selectors, enum maps, unit and currency tags, period and valid time, missing-value state per field); custom fields and an unmapped-field register per installation; assertions (named, with scope, tolerance with units, effect on failure); abstention with a closed list of reasons (unmapped enum, unresolved binding, applicability unknown, failed assertion, thin evidence). Rules: a recipe without assertions does not compile; a binding proposed by a model stays 'proposed' until a named person accepts it; the expression layer has no I/O, no clock and no unbounded loops. Write-back mappings are out of scope until day 7: reserve the keyword only. Mapper-2 and Mapper-3: test JSONata and CEL as the expression layer on the same 20 value transforms from the demo shop (dates, signs, Dr and Cr, currency, status maps) and post one table: cases passed, exact decimals kept, can a formula run forever, size added to the app. Atlas: write the specification as a standalone document, docs/mapping-language.md, separate from the code. First instance: rewrite the day 1 hand-written sales sheet recipe in the language and prove it produces the same rows. Every accept, reject and correction is written to the learning log. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "fleet",
      "do": "Mapper-1 posts the list of constructs. Open the diagram and trace it: the source, the company, the blanks, the reading rules, the recipe, the checks, the stop, the list of unmapped columns. Open the day 1 sales sheet next to it and point at one column for each idea."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "This is a check in Prove the engine room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Mapper-1, on screen, three things with the day 1 sales sheet recipe written in the language. (1) Remove its assertions and compile: it must fail and say why. (2) Add a row whose status value is not in the enum map: the recipe must abstain with the reason 'unmapped enum' and publish nothing for that row. (3) Add a column the recipe does not know: it must appear in the unmapped-field register. Then post the JSONata and CEL table.",
      "say": "A recipe that cannot check itself does not run. And when it meets something new, it stops and tells you the reason. That is the difference between a translator and a guess."
    }
  ],
  "gapIds": [
    "ONT-10",
    "ONT-11",
    "ONT-12"
  ]
}

Every set of books has a list of accounts in the owner's own names. Before any total is right, each account must be sorted into a class: sales, purchases, bank, tax, loans and so on. The AI proposes, you or your accountant decide, and every decision is logged. It is its own block because every money answer depends on it.

Source locator: DAYS[1].blocks[3]
{
  "what": "Every set of books has a list of accounts in the owner's own names. Before any total is right, each account must be sorted into a class: sales, purchases, bank, tax, loans and so on. The AI proposes, you or your accountant decide, and every decision is logged. It is its own block because every money answer depends on it.",
  "piece": "mapping",
  "architectureNodes": "acc=The accounts in your books, in your own names|list=One list of account classes, in three levels;prop=The AI proposes a class for each account and gives its reasons;you=You or your accountant: yes, no, or correct it|ask=Not sure: it asks one question;log=Every decision goes in the learning log, with who checked it;use=Sorted accounts feed revenue, costs and what is owed",
  "architectureEdges": "acc>prop;list>prop;prop>you;prop>~ask:if unsure;you>log;ask>log;log>use",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "Open any set of books and you find a list of accounts, named the way the owner likes. One shop calls it Sales Local. Another calls it Counter Sale. Before the companion can add up revenue or costs, it has to sort each account into a class. This is the hardest mapping in accounting, and the most valuable one. So it gets its own list of classes, its own evidence and its own score. The AI proposes a class and gives its reasons. You or your accountant say yes, no, or correct it. If it is not sure, it asks. And every decision is logged with who checked it, because a label checked by an accountant is worth more than a label guessed by a model."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Build room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Munim, start day 2 block 4 with Mapper-1, Design and Ledger: account classification as a first-class construct in the mapping language, not one more binding. (1) The target taxonomy: one list of account classes in three levels, in docs/account-classes.md, starting with the classes the first measures need (revenue, other income, cost of goods, expenses, receivables, payables, bank and cash, tax, loans, capital, stock). Show how Tally's default groups and Zoho Books account types map onto it. (2) Evidence per proposal: account name, parent group, debit or credit nature, and behaviour in the books. No amounts leave the machine. (3) The correction loop: proposed, accepted, rejected, corrected, abstained, each written to the learning log with the label grade (model, owner, accountant) and the recheck rule 'on a new account'. Party accounts (customers and suppliers by name) are classified by their group only, and their names never enter the sharing preview. (4) Metrics on the demo shop against your hand-labelled key: right, wrong, abstained, and wrong-but-accepted, which must be zero. Design: one screen where the owner sees each proposal with its reason and says yes, no, or corrects it. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "fleet",
      "do": "Munim posts the list of classes. Read the top level aloud. Open the demo shop's account list on screen and ask the viewers to guess the class of two odd ones before the companion does."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "This is a check in Prove the engine room. On screen, correct one proposal yourself, then paste:",
      "prompt": "Munim, post the score for the demo shop's chart of accounts against your hand-labelled key: right, wrong, abstained, out of the total. Then show the learning log line for the correction I just made, with its label grade. Warden: open the sharing preview for the last twenty classification events and answer yes or no, with evidence: does it contain any customer name, supplier name or amount?",
      "say": "It asked about the ones it was not sure of. That is what we want. And my correction is now a line in the log, on my machine, with my name on it."
    }
  ],
  "gapIds": [
    "ONT-13"
  ]
}

