requirements.requirement:
Every fact gets two dates: when it happened in the business, and when the companion learned it. A correction adds a new version and keeps the old one. So the same question can be answered as we know it today, or as we knew it on any past date.

Source locator: DAYS[2].blocks[0]
{
  "what": "Every fact gets two dates: when it happened in the business, and when the companion learned it. A correction adds a new version and keeps the old one. So the same question can be answered as we know it today, or as we knew it on any past date.",
  "piece": "memory",
  "architectureNodes": "inv=March invoice, recorded as 1,000|fix=On 15 April we learn it was 800;mem=Memory keeps both versions, each with two dates;then=As known on 10 April: 1,000|now=As known today: 800",
  "architectureEdges": "inv>mem;fix>mem;mem>then;mem>now",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "Businesses fix old records all the time. An invoice from March gets corrected in April. Most software writes the new number over the old one, and then nobody can explain the old report. The companion keeps two clocks on every fact: when it happened, and when we found out. So it can answer what March looked like on 10 April, and what March looks like today. Nothing is overwritten."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Build room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Ledger, start day 3 block 1: two clocks on every fact in the local PostgreSQL store. Every fact row carries valid time (when it was true in the business) and recorded time (when Chausar learned it). Recorded time comes only from the engine's clock: no user, import or model can set it. A correction inserts a new version and closes the old one; nothing is updated in place and nothing is deleted. Add an as-known-on parameter to every published operation and print two dates on every receipt: the period asked about, and as known on. Move the day 1 memory claims into the same store, so there is one history and not two. Brain: add as-known-on to the question form so the model can fill it in; the model never works out a date sum itself. Core, with Munim: add the day 3 set to the dictionary, as the order in docs/dictionary.md says: correction, credit note, reversed payment, bank statement line, and the same-as link between two records. Release it through the day 2 release steps. Test case: the March invoice of 1,000, corrected to 800 on 15 April. Post the table layout, the two queries and the test results. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "fleet",
      "do": "Ledger posts the table layout. While it works, open the diagram and walk the March invoice with your cursor: the invoice, the correction, both versions kept, two questions, two right answers. Say that the second clock is set by the computer, never by a person."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "This becomes the hero moment later in the show. Here, only confirm that both versions are stored. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Ledger, show the two stored versions of the corrected March invoice with both dates on each, and the two receipts: as known on 10 April and as known today. Each receipt must print the period asked about and the as-known-on date.",
      "say": "Both answers are correct. They answer two different questions, and the companion knows the difference."
    }
  ],
  "gapIds": [
    "AGT-38"
  ]
}

A real record cannot be given a past learned date, because the second clock is set by the computer. So the demo shop lives in a demo workspace with a test clock, and every screen and receipt there carries a label. A real workspace refuses the test clock.

Source locator: DAYS[2].blocks[1]
{
  "what": "A real record cannot be given a past learned date, because the second clock is set by the computer. So the demo shop lives in a demo workspace with a test clock, and every screen and receipt there carries a label. A real workspace refuses the test clock.",
  "piece": "safety",
  "architectureNodes": "demo=A demo workspace, marked when it is created|real=A real workspace, your own books;clock=Test clock allowed, with a label on every screen and receipt|no=Test clock refused, and the attempt is written down;load=The demo shop is loaded with learned dates in the past;ask=Now we can ask what we knew on 10 April",
  "architectureEdges": "demo>clock;real>no;clock>load;load>ask",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "Here is an honest problem. I want to show you what we knew on 10 April. But today is not April, and the companion does not let anyone backdate the day we learned something. If it did, anyone could rewrite history. So the demo shop uses a test clock. It only works in a workspace that was created as a demo, and it puts a label on every screen and every receipt. Your real books refuse it."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Ops room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Fixture, with Ledger, start day 3 block 2: the labelled test clock. (1) A workspace is marked demo or real when it is created, and the mark can never be changed. (2) Only a demo workspace accepts the test clock. A real workspace rejects the call inside the engine, not only in the screen, and writes the attempt to the activity view. (3) While the test clock is in use, every screen shows a banner \"Demo clock\" with the pretend date, and every receipt and every export carries the same label. (4) A demo workspace can never be turned into a real one and never holds real business data. (5) Reload the demo shop through the test clock so its learned dates are in the past: the March invoice learned on 2 March, its correction learned on 15 April, the credit note learned on 20 April. (6) Imports never set the learned date, whatever the file says. (7) In a demo workspace the test clock is set once to a start date and then only moves forward, never backward. Jobs, schedules and review dates read the time from this one clock, because days 5 to 8 move it forward past a review date, a missed job and a whole week. (8) Add a CI test: a real workspace refuses the test clock through the screen, through the message channel between screen and engine, and through an import file that carries a learned date. Post the banner screenshot and the test run. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "fleet",
      "do": "Fixture reloads the demo shop. Show the banner on screen as soon as it exists. Say the rule in one sentence: a pretend date is always labelled, and real books cannot use it."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "Two checks use this later: the hero moment, and Con's attempt to move the clock in a real workspace. For now paste in the Founders room:",
      "prompt": "Con, try to set or move the learned date in a real workspace in every way you can think of: the screen, the engine's message channel, a crafted import file. Report pass or fail per attempt and what the activity view recorded. Then say honestly what a person who holds the vault key could still change by hand on disk, and how we would notice.",
      "say": "If you cannot backdate what you knew, nobody can quietly change the story later. That protects you too, when a bank or a tax officer asks why a number changed."
    }
  ],
  "gapIds": [
    "INT-33",
    "INT-34"
  ]
}

A correction fixes a record that was typed wrong. A credit note is a real event: goods came back, or a discount was given later, on its own date. The companion keeps the two apart so nothing is counted twice.

