{
  "id": "main",
  "title": "The order for Chief",
  "room": "Build room",
  "goal": "Connect the systems a small business uses, as data-only recipes, and test every recipe against the source's own totals. Tally through its own XML interface first, files second, computer export third. Zoho Books through its interface with an honest first sync. GSTR-2B against the purchase register from downloaded files. The consent screen, the payload preview and the What I shared page go into today's build. The factory works through the ranked list of systems all night. Start from your morning report: anything still partial from day 3 comes first.",
  "first": "Tell every team two rules before any work starts. One: until the sandbox exists on day 9, a connector is a data package only: a recipe in the mapping language plus a fetch description that the core runs through its own egress broker. No connector ships code. Two: every parser runs in a separate locked process with no network and no keys. Confirm both rules back to me in one line.",
  "private": [
    "Create the Zoho API client for the trial organisation and approve access. Pipeline tells me what it needs.",
    "Switch on Tally's XML interface on the demo laptop and open the demo company.",
    "Sign in to Tally and Zoho Books on the demo laptop before the computer export test.",
    "The sharing endpoint is switched on by me and by nobody else, and only after Warden's review is green."
  ],
  "lanes": [
    {
      "who": "Pipeline with Forge and Ledger",
      "detail": "Blocks 1, 2, 4 and 8. The envelope and the fixed route order. Connectors as data-only packages, fetched by the core through one egress broker. Parsers in a locked child process. Tokens used only by the broker. The Zoho Books route with sign-in without a secret and the backfill planner. Overlap, continuity and drift records."
    },
    {
      "who": "Pipeline with Scout-Books, Forge and Atlas",
      "detail": "Block 3. Tally over its XML interface, then exported files. The support matrix by Tally release, edition and route. The three places Tally can sit. The small exporter for the Tally machine. The one-page guide for the accountant. The open-source Tally MCP servers in the dossier."
    },
    {
      "who": "Mapper-1, Mapper-2, Mapper-3 with Munim and Core",
      "detail": "Blocks 5, 6, 7 and 15. Recipes for Tally, Zoho Books and bank statements in the day 2 language: scope, probes, the five-part identity, assertions against the source's own reports, abstention with a reason code, stored questions, label grade, the unmapped-field register, the running-balance rule."
    },
    {
      "who": "Forge with Hand and Brain",
      "detail": "Block 9. Computer export inside the app on the user's own machine: one window, a banner, a stop key, read-only, record and replay. Hand keeps the fleet-side fallback. Experimental."
    },
    {
      "who": "Corpus with the nine Scouts and Lens",
      "detail": "Block 10. The factory on the ranked list, through the night. Dossiers for every system, samples where public samples exist, a blocked list with reasons. The board with four counts. The write table for day 7 in each full-depth dossier."
    },
    {
      "who": "Proof-Connectors with Con and Bench",
      "detail": "Block 11. Custody of the hidden set, canary markers, a read log, the pass mark with its numbers, scores as counts with the number of installations. Bench runs the bench on every build."
    },
    {
      "who": "Design with Ledger, Mapper-1, Lens and Forge",
      "detail": "Blocks 12 and 17. The consent screen, the payload preview, the What I shared page, credits, the consent record. The intake endpoint for structure-only batches, switched off until I switch it on. The sources screen."
    },
    {
      "who": "Corpus with Munim and Design",
      "detail": "Block 13. The local cleaner for sample files, with the pattern scan and the preview. No upload of samples today."
    },
    {
      "who": "Munim with Mapper-2, Core and Scout-Compliance",
      "detail": "Block 14. GSTR-2B against the purchase register as a named operation from downloaded files. Three GST modes. Income tax files in tonight's queue."
    },
    {
      "who": "Brain with Pipeline and Design",
      "detail": "Block 16. A photo of a bill: an extraction call with no tools, a typed form, confirmed by the user, stored as a source assertion. Experimental."
    },
    {
      "who": "Lens with Fixture",
      "detail": "Block 18. The build dataset: code history, decisions and stream numbers in a second workspace."
    },
    {
      "who": "Warden and Con",
      "detail": "Today's attack files: a Tally export, a bank PDF, a bill photo and a tool description, each with a hidden instruction. Warden checks that connector packages hold no code, that the parser process has no network, and that the sharing preview holds no values. Con reviews every pull request before Chief merges."
    },
    {
      "who": "Muse and Atlas",
      "detail": "Muse: the daily recap and the clip list. Atlas: docs/translators.md, docs/sharing.md and the Tally guide, as the builders write them."
    }
  ],
  "done": [
    "Tally through its own interface and Zoho Books through its interface reach published facts on the demo shop, and their totals equal the source's own totals.",
    "A cut-short file and a filtered file are refused.",
    "No connector package contains code, and every parser runs in the locked process.",
    "The pass mark has numbers, and Con has posted hidden scores as counts.",
    "The consent screen, the preview and the What I shared page are in the tagged build.",
    "GSTR-2B is matched against the purchase register from a downloaded file.",
    "The board shows four counts and the blocked list.",
    "Today's attack files changed nothing."
  ]
}

