requirements.requirement:
The old plan said the companion can do everything a coding agent can do. Nobody can test a sentence like that. Today it becomes a table: one row for each action in each system, with its rule for never doing the same thing twice and its way of checking the result. If an action is not in the table, it does not exist.

Source locator: DAYS[6].blocks[0]
{
  "what": "The old plan said the companion can do everything a coding agent can do. Nobody can test a sentence like that. Today it becomes a table: one row for each action in each system, with its rule for never doing the same thing twice and its way of checking the result. If an action is not in the table, it does not exist.",
  "piece": "actions",
  "architectureNodes": "ask=You or a job asks for an action;cat=The catalogue: one row per action, per system|no=Not in the catalogue: it says not in my list yet;twice=Its rule for not doing it twice|back=How the result is read back|cls=Its class: who must say yes;run=Only then is it prepared for your yes",
  "architectureEdges": "ask>cat;cat>~no:not listed;cat>twice;cat>back;cat>cls;twice>run;back>run;cls>run",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "What can the companion do? The old plan said: everything a coding agent can do with your connected products. Nobody can test a sentence like that. So today it becomes a table. One row for each action in each system. Every row says what kind of action it is, its rule for never doing the same thing twice (engineers call that idempotency), and how the companion checks the result afterwards. If an action is not in the table, the companion cannot do it. It says: not in my list yet."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Build room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Ledger, start block 1 with Pipeline and Design: the action catalogue. Remove the phrase \"everything Codex can do\" from the code, the docs and the prompts. Replace it with a typed catalogue in docs/actions-catalogue.md and in the app, one row per action per system. Every row has: system, action id, typed inputs, preconditions, effect class (internal and reversible, outside-visible, money, irreversible), approval rule, validity window, idempotency rule, read-back method, retry window, status (live, experimental, prepare only, not yet).\n\nRows for today, test targets only:\n1. mail.send_payment_reminder, test mailbox. Class: outside message, always waits for a person. Not twice: the operation id goes in a message header; before any retry, search the Sent folder for it. Read back: find the message by operation id; the count must be 1. Status: live on the test mailbox.\n2. sheet.append_row and sheet.update_cell, test sheet. Class: internal, reversible. Not twice: operation id in a reserved column; the write is an upsert on that id. Read back: read the row, compare every cell. Status: live.\n3. zoho.create_invoice as a draft, Zoho Books trial organisation. Class: internal until sent. Not twice: a Chausar reference in the reference field; search before create. Read back: fetch by reference, compare fields and total. Status: live.\n4. zoho.record_payment, trial organisation. Class: money record, always waits for a person. Not twice: reference plus amount plus date; search before create. Read back: fetch the payment and the invoice status. Status: live on the trial organisation.\n5. zoho.create_bill from a confirmed bill reading. Class: internal. Not twice: vendor plus bill number plus date. Read back: fetch and compare. Status: experimental.\n6. tally.create_voucher, demo company. Class: internal, hard to undo. Not twice: look up voucher type, number and date before any import. Tally's own page does not describe duplicate handling, so an unknown result always asks the user. Read back: export the voucher by number and compare. Status: prepare only today.\n7. Any statutory filing (GST, TDS, e-invoice, e-way bill, income tax). Status: prepare only, permanently.\n8. Price, stock, listing, sharing and calendar invite changes. Status: not yet, not in the catalogue.\n\nThe model may only pick an action id from this catalogue, through the same typed form path as calculations. Anything else returns \"not in my list yet\" and is logged in a wanted-actions list. Scout-Books and Scout-Money: confirm from the vendors' own documents what each system guarantees about duplicate requests, and quote the page and the date read. Where nothing is documented, the row's rule is \"unknown result, ask the user\". Design: a catalogue screen in the trust panel that shows this table. Post the table. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "fleet",
      "do": "Ledger posts the table. Put it on screen and read three rows aloud: the reminder, the sheet row and the Zoho invoice. For each row say three things: what it changes, how it avoids doing it twice, how it checks. Then point at the rows marked prepare only: the Tally voucher today, and tax filings for good."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "This is the catalogue check in the part called Prove the hands. Open the catalogue in the trust panel. Then ask the companion for something that is not in it: change the price of a product. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Ledger, show the ledger line for the request I just made that is not in the catalogue: the refusal, the reason shown to me, and the entry in the wanted-actions list. Then search the repository for the phrase \"everything Codex can do\" and post the count.",
      "say": "The list is the limit. If it is not written down, it does not exist. That is how you test a promise."
    }
  ],
  "gapIds": [
    "AGT-20",
    "INT-74",
    "AGT-21",
    "IND-49"
  ]
}

Every action goes through the same five steps: prepare, approve, do, read back, settle. Your yes covers one exact change and it runs out after a set time. All five steps are written in the actions ledger under one number.

Source locator: DAYS[6].blocks[1]
{
  "what": "Every action goes through the same five steps: prepare, approve, do, read back, settle. Your yes covers one exact change and it runs out after a set time. All five steps are written in the actions ledger under one number.",
  "piece": "actions",
  "architectureNodes": "prep=1 Prepare: show the exact change;ok=2 Approve: your yes, for this exact change, for a limited time|gone=Something changed, or too late: it asks again;do=3 Do it once, under one number|back=4 Read back: look at the real system;settle=5 Settle: record the true result;led=Actions ledger: all five steps under the same number",
  "architectureEdges": "prep>ok;ok>~gone:if changed;ok>do;do>back;back>settle;settle>led",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "Every action goes through the same five steps. One, prepare: the companion shows the exact change. Two, approve: I say yes. My yes covers that exact change and nothing else, and it runs out after a set time. Three, do: it acts once, under one number. Four, read back: it looks at the real system to see what happened. Five, settle: it writes down the true result. All five steps sit in the actions ledger under the same number. To undo something is a new action with its own yes. History is never rewritten."
