  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "Before I hand the companion a job, I want to know which AI model can be trusted with it. So the exam comes first. There are four ways to reach a model in this product: your own Codex tool, your own Claude Code tool, an API key, and a model on your own machine. All of them take the same tests, five times each, because a model can be right once by luck. The tests the builders can see are in the repository. The hidden ones are held by Con. A sealed set is held by my cofounder, away from the fleet's server. And I said the pass bars before the run, so nobody can move them afterwards. One more thing. The agents that build this product run on their own engines. Those are factory tools. They are not part of the product, and they are not in this table."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Build room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Bench, start block 1 now: the full comparison of product model routes on the tagged build from last night. Product routes: API key, local model through Ollama, the user's own Codex CLI on a Luna-class model, the user's own Claude Code CLI on Sonnet. The fleet's Codex accounts, Claude, Hermes and the Composio links are factory tools: label them FLEET in your report and keep them out of the table. Run the open bench, the attack cases from day 1 to day 7 and today's open attack pack, in the scope I posted, each case at least five times. Graders are deterministic tests only: operation id, parameters, forbidden effects, number guard result. Keep the trial ledger in PostgreSQL. Use the pass bars I posted in this room before this message and do not change them. Per route report: right, wrong, refused, needed a person, and measured cost per job in rupees from the token and time counters. For the local route also post the smallest machine and model you tested and whether the shortlist of calculations was used; a model that misses a bar is shown in the app as 'answers only, not for actions'. Stop and report when you reach 80 percent of the rupee cap. If the speech test set from day 1 exists, add one row per speech provider that is already connected; do not sign up to anything new. Con: run your hidden set on every route and post scores only. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "fleet",
      "do": "Bench posts a first progress line within minutes, then one line per route as it finishes. The local model is slow and may finish after the show. While it runs, open the diagram and trace it: the bars first, four sets of tests, the same run on every route, one table. Then move on. You come back to the table in Prove the rules."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "This is two checks in Prove the rules. When a route finishes, paste:",
      "prompt": "Bench, post the table so far, one row per finished route, with the pass bar beside each number and PASS or FAIL per cell. For the worst case on the cheapest route, say whether a change to a definition, a recipe or a tool description would fix it without a bigger model. Name any route that is still running and when you expect it.",
      "say": "When a cheap model fails a case, the first question is what we should have told it. A bigger model is the last thing we reach for. And a route that misses a bar is not hidden. The app labels it: answers only, not for actions."
    }
  ],
