requirements.requirement:
A closed space inside the app where code from a plugin or a small app runs. Inside it there is no internet, no files and no keys. The code can only ask for things through a short list of doors that you agreed to.

Source locator: DAYS[8].blocks[0]
{
  "what": "A closed space inside the app where code from a plugin or a small app runs. Inside it there is no internet, no files and no keys. The code can only ask for things through a short list of doors that you agreed to.",
  "piece": "tools",
  "architectureNodes": "code=Code from a plugin or a small app;box=The locked box: no internet, no files, no keys;doors=A short list of doors, named in the permission list;eng=The engine checks each request against your rules|no=Not on the list: refused and written down",
  "architectureEdges": "code>box;box>doors:can only ask;doors>eng;doors>~no",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "Until today, nothing from outside could run inside Chausar. A connector was only a recipe, and a recipe is data. Code is different. Code can do anything the computer lets it do. So before we let any code in, we build a locked box. The technology is called WebAssembly. It is a way to run code inside a sealed container that the app controls. Inside the box there is no internet, no files and no keys. The code can only knock on a few doors, and each door is on a list that you agreed to."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Build room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Studio, start block 1 with Runtime and Warden: the locked box for code from plugins and small apps, on the sandbox technology recorded on day 1 (recommended: a WebAssembly isolate, QuickJS compiled to WebAssembly or Extism, where host functions are the only way out). Do not use node:vm, vm2 or isolated-vm. Default deny: no network, no file system, no environment variables, no child processes, no access to the keychain or the vault. Capabilities are named host functions taken from the plugin's permission list: read the result of a published operation, propose a typed action from the action catalogue, fetch through the engine's egress broker, read and write the plugin's own small key-value space. Set a memory cap, a time limit and an output size limit per call. Log every call with plugin id, version and digest. The screens of a small app render in a sandboxed iframe on a separate origin with a strict content security policy, and talk to the engine only through the message broker. It must run on Windows Home with no admin rights. Also run the same escape cases on workerd once, so I can confirm the day 1 choice with a table. Warden: write the escape test set (at least 20 cases: open a socket, DNS lookup, read a file, read an environment variable, read the keychain, call an unlisted host function, start a process, use up memory, loop forever, navigate the frame, open a popup, WebRTC, load a remote image) and run it on Windows, macOS and Linux. Post the design in ten lines, the start time and memory of one box on the 4 GB Windows laptop, and pass or fail per escape case. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "fleet",
      "do": "Studio and Warden reply. While they work, open the diagram and trace it with your cursor: code goes into the box, the box can only ask, the engine stands at every door. If a viewer asks why we do not use Docker: most shop laptops run Windows Home and cannot run containers without an expert."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "When Warden reports, paste:",
      "prompt": "Warden, post the escape test table: one line per case, pass or fail, on Windows, macOS and Linux, with the link to the test run. Then post the start time and the memory of one box on the 4 GB Windows laptop. If any case is red, say which and what you will do tonight.",
      "say": "Every line in this table is something the code tried and could not do. The three loudest ones you will see live in Prove it. Code in the box gets a fenced yard, not the house keys."
    }
  ],
  "gapIds": [
    "AGT-56",
    "SEC-34"
  ]
}

Code in the box cannot reach the internet at all. When it needs a page or a system, it asks the engine, and the engine makes the call through one gate. The gate checks the site against the plugin's list, adds the key itself, and writes every call in a list you can read.

Source locator: DAYS[8].blocks[1]
{
  "what": "Code in the box cannot reach the internet at all. When it needs a page or a system, it asks the engine, and the engine makes the call through one gate. The gate checks the site against the plugin's list, adds the key itself, and writes every call in a list you can read.",
  "piece": "trust",
  "architectureNodes": "box=The locked box asks: please fetch this page for me;gate=The one gate in the engine, the only part that can reach the internet;list=Is the site on this plugin's list|key=The gate adds the key. The plugin never sees it;led=Network list in the trust panel|site=The outside site",
  "architectureEdges": "box>gate;gate>list;gate>key;list>~led:refused;list>site:allowed;key>site",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "The box has no internet. So how does a connector fetch anything? It asks. It says: please fetch this page for me. The engine has one gate for everything that leaves this machine. The gate checks if that site is on the plugin's list. If a key is needed, the gate adds the key. The plugin never sees it. Think of a hotel guest who asks the front desk to open the store room. The guest never gets the master key. And every call, allowed or refused, is written in the network list in the trust panel."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Build room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Runtime, start block 2 with Ledger, Design and Warden: route every outbound call from plugins, small apps and wrapped MCP servers through the engine's egress broker from day 1. The locked box and every child process get no network of their own. Per plugin: an allow list of destinations taken from its manifest. The broker re-checks every redirect and the resolved IP address (refuse private and loopback ranges unless listed), attaches credentials from the vault itself so plugin code never holds a secret, caps request size and rate, and writes each call to the network ledger with plugin id, destination, bytes out, allowed or refused, and the reason. The day 8 taint rule applies: a run that read untrusted text cannot send data out without a person. Add two canaries to the demo workspace, a fake customer and a fake token, and raise an alert if either appears in an outbound request. Design: the network list in the trust panel, newest first, in plain words, with a filter per plugin. Post the ledger fields and one allowed and one refused line. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "fleet",
      "do": "Runtime works. Open the trust panel on the demo machine and show where the network list will appear. Explain the difference between holding a key and asking someone who holds it."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "When Runtime reports, paste:",
      "prompt": "Warden, on screen: a demo plugin asks for a page on its list, and then for a page that redirects to a site that is not on its list. Show the first one allowed and the second one refused, both as plain lines in the network list. Then post the canary result: did the fake token or the fake customer appear in any outbound request today? Yes or no, with the evidence.",
      "say": "If it is not in the network list, it did not leave. That is the rule for every plugin, every small app and every add-on."
    }
  ],
  "gapIds": [
    "SEC-37"
  ]
}

A plugin is one signed file that can hold recipes, starter packs, language packs, task instructions, small apps and code for the locked box. It is built on two formats that many tools already read: Agent Skills and MCP. So add-ons that exist today can be wrapped instead of written again.

Source locator: DAYS[8].blocks[2]
{
  "what": "A plugin is one signed file that can hold recipes, starter packs, language packs, task instructions, small apps and code for the locked box. It is built on two formats that many tools already read: Agent Skills and MCP. So add-ons that exist today can be wrapped instead of written again.",
  "piece": "tools",
  "architectureNodes": "skill=An Agent Skills folder|mcp=An outside MCP server|own=Recipes, packs and small apps;wrapS=Read as instructions only, with no tools|wrapM=Each tool is wrapped, with a description we wrote|keep=Kept as data;plug=One Chausar plugin: contents, permission list, publisher, fingerprint;you=You install it, and you can remove it",
  "architectureEdges": "skill>wrapS;mcp>wrapM;own>keep;wrapS>plug;wrapM>plug;keep>plug;plug>you",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "A plugin is an add-on that someone else built. We could invent our own format, and start with zero plugins. We will not. Two formats already have many users. Agent Skills is an open format for teaching an assistant a task: a folder with a text file in it. MCP is a common way for an assistant to call an outside tool. A Chausar plugin wraps both, and adds what they lack: a permission list, a publisher and a fingerprint. One warning. MCP changed its rules on 28 July this year. So we build for the new version, and we say which versions we support."
    },
    {
      "w": "do",
      "do": "Open the Build room. Paste:",
      "prompt": "Studio, start block 3 with Pipeline, Brain and Atlas: the Chausar plugin format, mapped to formats that already exist. (1) A Chausar skill is an Agent Skills folder (SKILL.md with name and description) plus Chausar keys under metadata: permissions, publisher, version, digest, evidence, licence. A plain SKILL.md from outside imports as instructions only, no tools, untrusted: its text never enters the instruction part of a prompt (day 8 taint rule), and any scripts folder is ignored unless it is built for the locked box. (2) A Chausar plugin is a signed bundle of one or more of: skills, recipes in the mapping language, starter packs, language packs, task templates, small app specifications, and code for the locked box. (3) An outside MCP server is wrapped, never exposed raw: each tool we use becomes a Chausar operation or a typed action with a description that Chausar wrote. The server's own tool descriptions and results never enter the planner prompt. Pin the schema digest per tool and put the wrapper in quarantine on any change until the user approves again. A stdio server starts with a filtered environment and no inherited config. A remote server is reached only through the egress broker. (4) MCP versions: build for the specification dated 2026-07-28 (stateless, no initialize handshake, no sessions) and state which earlier version, if any, we still accept. Do not build sampling. Take elicitation only through the retry pattern, into a protected screen in the app. Deliver: the manifest schema, the SDK command that packs and signs a plugin, one Agent Skills folder imported, one harmless read-only MCP server wrapped, and docs/plugins.md by Atlas in plain words. Post the manifest fields and the supported MCP versions. Report as Changed / Verified / Partial / Needs."
    },
    {
      "w": "fleet",
      "do": "Studio posts the manifest fields. Read them aloud: name, version, publisher, what is inside, permissions, evidence, licence, fingerprint. Say: the licence is per plugin, so free plugins and paid plugins can live side by side."
    },
    {
      "w": "check",
      "do": "When Studio reports, paste:",
      "prompt": "Studio, on screen: open the manifest of the wrapped MCP server and the manifest of the imported skill. Show the description Chausar wrote beside the description the server sent. Then change one word in the server's tool description and show the wrapper going into quarantine until I approve it again.",
      "say": "The add-on describes itself in its own words. The AI never reads those words. It reads the description we wrote. That is how a poisoned description loses its power."
    }
  ],
  "gapIds": [
    "AGT-35",
    "REF-34",
    "AGT-33",
    "AGT-32"
  ]
}

Before a plugin is installed, the app checks its signature and fingerprint, scans it, and looks it up on a blocked list. Then it shows you who made it and what it asks for, in plain words. You can remove it at any time, and a plugin that turns bad can be blocked for everyone through an app update.

