{
  "what": "Before a plugin is installed, the app checks its signature and fingerprint, scans it, and looks it up on a blocked list. Then it shows you who made it and what it asks for, in plain words. You can remove it at any time, and a plugin that turns bad can be blocked for everyone through an app update.",
  "piece": "tools",
  "architectureNodes": "file=A plugin file from someone else;sig=Check the signature and the fingerprint|scan=Scan for loose code and hidden instructions|bl=Look it up on the blocked list;ask=Install screen: who made it and what it asks for, in plain words;yes=You say yes: it runs with those permissions only|rem=Remove or block: permissions are gone at once",
  "architectureEdges": "file>sig;file>scan;file>bl;sig>ask;scan>ask;bl>ask;ask>yes;yes>rem:later",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "w": "say",
      "say": "In one public registry of add-ons, about one in eight entries was found to be harmful. So a plugin from a stranger is not trusted. Four things happen before it runs. The app checks the signature: who made this, and has the file changed since. It scans the file: any code that is not built for the locked box is refused. It looks the plugin up on a blocked list, which comes with every app update. Then it shows you an install screen in plain words: who made it, what it may read, what it may do, which sites it may reach. Everything is off until you say yes."
    },
    {
