  "first": "Post the decision record for the sandbox technology from day 1 and the list of effect classes from day 7. Every lane below depends on them. If either is missing, say so now and I decide on camera.",
  "private": [
    "Connect the WhatsApp test number and the provider key. I type them myself into the settings of the relay, off camera. No bot ever receives the key or the number.",
    "Pair the two outside assistants with the numbers server on the demo machine. The pairing codes are shown once and stay off camera."
  ],
  "lanes": [
    {
      "who": "Studio with Runtime and Warden",
      "detail": "Block 1. The locked box on the technology recorded on day 1: a WebAssembly isolate where host functions are the only way out, and a sandboxed frame on a separate origin for the screens of small apps. No network, no files, no keys by default. Warden writes the escape test set and runs it on Windows, macOS and Linux."
    },
    {
      "who": "Runtime with Ledger, Design and Warden",
      "detail": "Block 2. Every outgoing call from plugins, small apps and wrapped add-ons goes through the egress broker from day 1: a per-plugin allow list, brokered secrets, redirect and address checks, canaries, and the network list in the trust panel."
    },
    {
      "who": "Studio with Pipeline, Brain and Atlas",
      "detail": "Block 3. The plugin format mapped to Agent Skills and MCP, the SDK command that packs and signs a plugin, one skill imported as instructions only, one outside MCP server wrapped with descriptions we wrote, the MCP version of 2026-07-28 pinned, and plugin docs in plain words."
    },
    {
      "who": "Studio with Forge, Warden and Design",
      "detail": "Block 4. Install, verify, remove and block: signed manifests, pinned publisher keys, four trust levels, the scan on install, the install screen in plain words, the signed revocation list inside app updates, and the offline rule I decide today."
    },
    {
      "who": "Proof-Connectors with Mapper-1, Core and Analyst",
      "detail": "Block 5. A qualification state on every recipe and on every receipt. Community recipes start unqualified and pass the same holdout gate as ours. Reports for a bank or a CA warn when a number rests on an unqualified recipe."
    },
    {
      "who": "Munim with Voice, Brain and Bench",
      "detail": "Block 6. The language pack format and the Hinglish pack with its own test set. The number guard reads Hinglish number words."
    },
    {
      "who": "Design with Brain, Core and Studio",
      "detail": "Block 7. The app maker: one line becomes a typed app specification bound to published operations and typed actions, validated by software, with stale marking when a definition changes."
    },
    {
      "who": "Ledger with Design and Studio",
      "detail": "Block 8. One generated system that keeps its own records: the customer follow-up book, stored in the business memory with two clocks, labelled experimental."
    },
    {
      "who": "Brain with Ledger, Runtime and Warden",
      "detail": "Block 9. Chausar as a read-only MCP server on the user's machine: four tools, local transport only, a pairing token per client, the disclosure check on every result, every call in the activity view."
    },
    {
      "who": "Voice with Ledger and Design",
      "detail": "Block 10. Simple approvals by WhatsApp and mail for the owner only: approved templates with buttons, no free chat, no figures in messages, one-time tokens bound to the action digest with an expiry, stored opt-in, HELP and STOP, message cost shown."
    },
    {
      "who": "Forge with Runtime, Voice and Warden",
      "detail": "Block 11. The relay: sealed envelopes only, collected by the desktop when it is awake, content-free logs, docs/relay.md. Nothing is deployed to a new host and nothing is paid for without my yes."
    },
    {
      "who": "Pipeline with Voice, Mapper-2 and Warden",
      "detail": "Block 12. Document intake by WhatsApp, experimental: a forwarded photo or PDF enters the day 4 and day 3 paths as data, confirmed by the user in the app."
    },
    {
      "who": "Con, Warden, Bench, Proof-Product and Fixture",
      "detail": "Con and Warden make today's attack files: a plugin with hidden instructions and a loose script, a plugin that tries to leave the locked box, a poisoned MCP tool description, a forwarded approval, a bill photo and a PDF with hidden text. Fixture adds them to the demo shop. Bench runs the bench on every build, once in the app and once through the numbers server. Con runs the hidden set and reviews every pull request. Proof-Product installs the tagged build on clean machines and installs one plugin from its file using the docs only."
    },
    {
      "who": "Atlas, Muse and Lens",
      "detail": "Atlas: docs/plugins.md, docs/relay.md, the language pack guide, and the day 9 line of the fork guide with Studio. Muse: the recap and the clip list. Lens: new event types in the learning log for app requests, plugin permission decisions and approval outcomes by channel, structure only, no values."
    },
    {
      "who": "Factory team (Corpus, 9 Scouts, Mappers, Proof-Connectors, Lens)",
      "detail": "The connector factory keeps running. From today the Mappers pack each qualified recipe as a data-only plugin with its evidence. Report any change to the four counts: researched, sampled, mapped, qualified."
    }
  ],
  "done": [
    "The locked box passes every escape case on Windows, Mac and Linux, with the test run linked.",
    "Every outgoing call of a plugin goes through the one gate and shows as a line in the network list.",
    "A plugin installs only with a valid signature, shows its permissions in plain words, and can be removed and blocked, with log lines for each.",
    "No plugin code runs outside the locked box: the install scan refuses it, shown by a test run.",
    "A community recipe shows not yet tested on every receipt until it passes the hidden test.",
    "A small app made from one line cannot skip approval and goes out of date when a definition changes.",
