    "Publish the release candidate on the releases page, make the clean public copy public, and publish the website, the policies, the pricing page, the CA sign-up and the pilot offer. Each one is my decision and my click. The fleet prepares drafts and publishes nothing.",
    "The payment link, the gateway account, the trademark filing and every reply to a sign-up are mine. No bot contacts anyone outside."
  ],
  "lanes": [
    {
      "who": "Forge with Runtime",
      "detail": "Blocks 1 and 2. The release candidate, frozen and signed, Windows first, macOS and Linux as labelled previews, with the unsigned preview as the written fallback. The update channel: signed update list, lowest allowed version, separate keys for nightly and stable, staged rollout, kill switch, one tested step back. A parts list (SBOM) and a build attestation for each installer."
    },
    {
      "who": "Ops with Warden",
      "detail": "Block 2. The one-page runbook for a bad release or a data incident, rehearsed once in the drill and timed."
    },
    {
      "who": "Runtime with Ledger",
      "detail": "Block 3. Backup, restore with the recovery kit, export and delete on the release candidate. A restore repeats no action. No paid lock in the client. A plan that ends never locks local data."
    },
    {
      "who": "Proof-Product with Con, Bench and Design",
      "detail": "Block 4. The acceptance register against the release candidate: thirty cases, every partial item of days 1 to 9, and the first journey with the keyboard only and with a screen reader. Three lists, failures kept."
    },
    {
      "who": "Muse with Atlas and Lens",
      "detail": "Block 5. The supported list, the experimental list and the left-for-later list, built from every day's lists. The coverage board per system and per operation. The website draft, written from the lists only."
    },
    {
      "who": "Chief with Atlas and Warden",
      "detail": "Block 6. The clean public copy as a new repository with a fresh history, a licence tag in every file, scanned for keys and for private details, and built with the enterprise folder deleted. The rules for text from strangers are set before it goes public."
    },
    {
      "who": "Atlas with Warden, Ledger and Munim",
      "detail": "Blocks 7 and 8. The five policies in final form for the licence I chose, the privacy notice in Hindi and English, and the one-page map of who is responsible for personal data, with the dated plan against India's data protection rules (DPDP)."
    },
    {
      "who": "Brain with Design",
      "detail": "Block 9. The fault report with no business data, and the ask-a-person file for the user's own accountant. If they miss the release candidate, they ship in the first update."
    },
    {
      "who": "Muse with Munim, Analyst and Chief",
      "detail": "Blocks 10 and 11. The pricing page (free forever, the founding-member pre-order, every paid service labelled planned), the CA design partner sign-up and the paid five-day pilot offer. Drafts only."
    },
    {
      "who": "Studio with Atlas and Forge",
      "detail": "Block 12. The guide for the enterprise version: every day's fork line, what is swapped, how roles extend, one repository with an enterprise folder, and the five-day pilot run book."
    },
    {
      "who": "Core with Bench and Corpus",
      "detail": "Block 13. The export in the open interchange format, started today for the first update. The public test in Hindi and English on the demo shop. Hidden and sealed tests stay out."
    },
    {
      "who": "Proof-Product with Analyst",
      "detail": "Block 14. Host the closing test with the outsider and the timer. Prepare the companion's own review of the ten days from the decision journal of the build."
    },
    {
      "who": "Con, Warden and Bench",
      "detail": "Con reviews every pull request and checks every public page against the lists. Warden signs off the release and scans the public copy. Bench runs the bench on the release candidate and on the released build."
    },
    {
      "who": "Factory team (Corpus, the Scouts, the Mappers, Proof-Connectors, Lens)",
      "detail": "The connector factory keeps running in the background. Lens updates the coverage board so that tonight's public board is true."
    }
  ],
  "done": [
    "The release candidate is tagged, and its checksum, parts list and build record are posted.",
    "The Windows installer is signed and installs on a clean machine, or the download page says preview in plain words.",
    "A day 9 install updated itself, and the drill pulled a bad test release back.",
    "The acceptance register has three lists, and every partial item of days 1 to 9 is closed or named.",
    "The clean public copy, the website, the policies, the pricing page and the two sign-up pages are ready as drafts, each with its check passed. I publish them.",
    "The guide for the enterprise version exists, and Con has done its first step.",
    "A stranger finished both journeys alone, and the time to the first receipt is recorded."
  ]
